# THIS MODULE PUTS NOTHING THROUGH GIT LFS, and that is a decision rather than
# an omission. The repository's own .gitattributes still routes the plugins, the
# recorded takes and the meshes through it - those exist nowhere else, and a
# recipe cannot reproduce them. Weights are the opposite kind of thing.
#
# WHY THE MODEL FORMATS ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT FROM THE LIST BELOW.
#
# The obvious move is to keep "*.bin filter=lfs" as a safety net, so that a
# weight file committed by accident becomes a pointer instead of two gigabytes
# in the history. It is the wrong move, and the reason is worth the paragraph:
#
#   WITHOUT the rule, GitHub refuses any file over 100 MB at push time. The
#   mistake costs one rejected push and is over.
#
#   WITH the rule, the push SUCCEEDS. Two gigabytes land in the remote's LFS
#   store, count against the allowance - ten gibibytes on a free account, so two
#   gigabytes is a fifth of it - and, measured rather than
#   feared - CANNOT BE REMOVED by deleting the file afterwards. An LFS object
#   stays in the remote's storage after it is gone from every commit; GitHub's
#   own answer is to delete and recreate the repository, or to write to support.
#
# So the "safety net" turns a loud, free, reversible failure into a quiet,
# billed, permanent one. The net is weights/.gitignore instead, and the hard
# limit behind it is GitHub's.

# SHA256SUMS is TEXT and must never be an LFS pointer. It is the one file that
# has to be readable in a plain clone - it is what says whether a downloaded
# weight file is the right one - and a checksum file behind a pointer is a
# checksum nobody can check.
SHA256SUMS      text eol=lf -filter -diff -merge
